From 7b45b7c021916ea451bb7ac57a72cb83548ffe06 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nicole Muzzin <nicks@fuckin.email>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 04:56:38 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] svg: embed SVG <image> targets, cross-svg paint servers,
 per-svg raster cache

Inline SVG is rasterized from a standalone serialization, so everything it
references has to be carried into that document. Three gaps showed up on
real sites:

An `<image href>` pointing at an SVG document (target.com's logo) painted
nothing: only raster targets were re-embedded. The image cache now keeps the
SVG text and the root's preserveAspectRatio (when it has a viewBox) next to
the parsed tree, and the serializer embeds the document as a data: URL. The
<image>'s preserveAspectRatio is replaced to reproduce Chrome, which fits the
nested viewBox with the referenced root's own preserveAspectRatio, stretches
a document without a viewBox, and only honours the <image>'s value when it is
`none`. The href is now substituted through the serializer's attribute
rewrite instead of inserting a sibling <image> into the DOM: inserting into a
nested element (an <image> inside a <g>) re-invalidated the svg on every
serialization, so it re-serialized endlessly and never painted. The rewrite
also drops `xlink:href` when a plain `href` is present: it serializes without
its prefix, and the duplicate `href` made the whole document ill-formed
(blank), which is what Target's markup (both attributes) hit.

Paint servers defined in another <svg> were only pulled in when named by a
`fill`/`stroke` attribute. A fill/stroke set by a stylesheet rule or inline
style, and gradients/patterns inheriting from another one through `href`,
rendered missing. The external-reference pass now also follows the computed
fill/stroke and gradient/pattern href chains.

Identical inline <svg>s (repeated icons) serialized to the same data: URL and
shared one image-cache entry, while eviction is per svg element: when one of
them re-serialized (a hover or class change), it evicted the entry and its
rasterizations from under the others, which then painted nothing until
something else damaged them. Each serialization now carries the element's
id in a leading comment, so every svg owns its entry.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
